===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|trien|t=to separate out, sift, choose, select, evaluate, try a legal case}}, from {{der|en|xno|trier}}, {{m|xno|triher}}, {{m|xno|triere|t=to divide, separate, choose, select, prove, determine, try a case}}, {{der|en|fro|trier|t=to choose, pick out or separate from others, sift, cull}}, of {{unc|en|nocap=1}} origin. Cognate with {{cog|oc|triar|t=to choose, sort, scrutinise, peel}}, {{cog|ca|triar|t=to pick, choose, decide}}. Suggested to be derived from {{der|en|la-vul|*trītāre|t=to crush, grind, trample, wear out}}, itself derived from {{cog|la-cla|trītus|t=rubbed, worn down, pulverised}}, the past participle of {{m|la|tero|terō, terere|t=to rub, wear down, trample}}, though this derivation is incompatible with the Occitan form. Additionally, the shift in meaning from "rub, crush, trample" to "pick out, choose, cull" is difficult to explain. One suggestion is that the semantic shift might have originated from a Latin phrase {{m|la||*granum terere}} {{gl|"to tread the corn (in threshing)"; compare {{cog|la|trītūra|t=rubbing, chafing, friction" also "threshing}}}}, which has a parallel in the modern {{m+|fr|trier le grain|t=to sort the grain}}. Alternatively, perhaps derived from {{der|en|la-vul|*trīāre}}, a metathetic alteration of {{m|la-vul|*tīrāre|t=to tear off, pull, draw}}, whence also {{der|en|fro|tirer|t=to draw, pull, pluck, tug, peck at, extract}}, {{cog|oc|tirar|t=to take, draw, retrieve, remove, extract}}.

Replaced native {{cog|enm|cunnen|t=to try}} (from {{cog|ang|cunnian}}), {{cog|enm|fandien|t=to try, prove}} (from {{cog|ang|fandian}}), and {{cog|enm|costnien|t=to try, tempt, test}} (from {{cog|ang|costnian}}).

